::ENG4_U06_L03_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Research brief: A college success office found that 72 percent of surveyed students felt more prepared for interviews after formal presentation practice, and faculty reported that oral explanation helped students clarify claims and evidence. Which option best prepares this research for oral presentation? {
~A. List every research detail without explaining why it matters.#Incorrect. Listing details creates information overload. A strong presentation selects relevant evidence and explains how it supports the claim and audience purpose.
=B. State a focused claim, select credible evidence, explain significance, and acknowledge limits accurately.#Correct. This answer best applies the lesson skill with clear reasoning, appropriate evidence, and audience awareness.
~C. Use only personal opinion because oral presentations should sound casual.#Incorrect. Personal opinion alone does not establish credibility. Oral communication in this unit must use focused claims, evidence, and reasoning.
~D. Ignore limitations so the presentation sounds stronger.#Incorrect. Ignoring limits overstates the evidence. A credible speaker explains what the evidence shows and acknowledges reasonable limits.
}

